  https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Trapezoidal_rule
  
  We now have a discrete input signal. We want to apply a filter to it.
  We have block diagrams to help us see equations as signal paths.
  Additions and multiplications are pretty straightforward.
  But there are integrators sometimes.

  We've seen (Chapter III, #1) that integrating a function's rate of change rebuilds the function.
  But if we integrate the function itself, we get something else.
  (Antiderivative obviously, but that's not my target here.)

  /*
    In what follows I loosely use "function" to mean "function's output value".
    It's not semantically rigorous but it avoids the confusion between function output and output signal.
    I.e.: if we integrate our input signal over time, the function's input is time and its output is the input signal...
  */

  Think graphical.
  At each infinitesimal step, we multiply the function by a small increment in its variable (differential).
  On a graph, the differential is a horizontal (infinitesimal) segment; the function's value is a vertical segment.
  This figure is called a rectangle.
  And multiplying one side by the other is how we measure the area of a rectangle.

  So each step of an integral is the area of a rectangle.
  And the integration itself is the sum of all the rectangles.

  This produces an approximation of the area under the curve.
  And it's as precise as the differential is small.

  Except here our variable is time and our differential is T (the sampling period, the length of a sample).
  It's measurable and therefore not really infinitesimal.

  So the rectangle is a nice start, but it could easily benefit from some optimisation.
  
  What follows is very simple, on the sine qua non condition that you think graphical.
  Did I mention THINK GRAPHICAL?

  https://www.desmos.com/calculator/g6ozuv0whk

  Between samples, information is lost.
  But we can do better than hard-stepped jumps: the shortest way between two points is a straight line.
  And two points are all a straight line requires to be defined.

  So, knowing the previous amplitude (which we do), we would better approximate the lost curve by drawing a straight line
  to the current amplitude.

  The figure that previously was a rectangle is now a right triangle on top of a rectangle.
  (If you cannot picture it, open the last Desmos link.)
  This figure is called a TRAPEZOID.
  The method of integration we're using is called the trapezoidal rule.
  The integrator we're building is called a TRAPEZOIDAL INTEGRATOR.

  A right triangle is half a rectangle. Second easiest area to calculate: (opposite + adjacent) / 2.
  We don't need the hypotenuse. So we already have all the sides we need. Sorry Pythagoras, maybe next time.

  Let's name stuff:

    #  T is the sampling period (1 / f_s)
    #  x [n] is our current amplitude
    #  x [n - 1] is our previous amplitude

  The rectangle's horizontal side is T, its vertical side is x [n - 1].
  The triangle's adjacent and opposite sides are interchangeable depending on which angle we consider.
  Let's be arbitrary for the sake of simplicity: adjacent is T, opposite is  x [n]  -  x [n - 1].

  So the area of the rectangle is:

    T * x [n - 1]

  and the area of the right triangle is:

     T * ( x [n]  -  x [n - 1] )
    -----------------------------
                 2
  
