#04  But wait! That's a problem...
===============================================================================================

  You bet it is.
  And it's harder to fix than you might expect.

  The non-linearity of the frequency response warping is as negligible as the frequency is far from Nyquist.
  So high sampling rates help.

  Applying the inverse mapping to the whole system would make it non-linear, that is: no longer LTI -- so not a solution.

  As far as I know, two people came up with open-source solutions:

    # Martin Vicanek (2016):  https://vicanek.de/articles/BiquadFits.pdf

    # Yuriy Ivanstov (2025):  https://ivantsovy.com/research/paper1.pdf    and    https://ivantsovy.com/research/paper2.pdf

  Of course I'll try to come up with my own fix in some future chapter.

  Right now we'll use the most common workaround, which at decent sampling rate (88.2 kHz or 96 kHz), produces very negligible artefacts.
  In fact if you've ever used a digital filter (in a synth, an equaliser, a compressor's side chain, a virtual channel strip, a car's radio,
  a HiFi system, a phone......) -- and didn't get annoyed by how the high frequency response was being cramped -- you've already approved this
  workaround.

  The technique is called PRE-WARPING.
  It consists in applying the inverse mapping to one frequency of interest, and re-express the cutoff accordingly.
  The cutoff remains a constant linear gain: its non-linear transformation happens in the parameters update, not in the signal path.
  This way we have complete control over the exact gain and phase shift of one partial.
  This partial is usually at the cutoff frequency, because usually, there's a user-facing parameter labelled "Cutoff (Hz)" and we want it to
  make sense.
  But it's a common practice regarding a design decision. Nothing mandatory.
  For instance my old Fender Super Reverb has a knob that says "Treble" ranging from 1 to 10 (no Stairway to Heaven, no Spinal Tap jokes).
  If I were to model its tone-stack's circuit (which might happen in a future chapter) I wouldn't need a precise cutoff and could experiment
  with pre-warping to optimise the curve -- if the cutoff happens to be the best pre-warping point, at least it wouldn't be chosen arbitrarily.

  So I suggest we find a general pre-warping formula for an undefined point. Of course the only parameter directly related to frequency in our
  block diagrams and transfer functions is the cutoff, so it would have to be re-expressed according to our transformation.
  This suggests that for the special case where the pre-warping point is the cutoff, the formula should become simpler. We'll see.

  If our digital filter receives a partial  0 < ω_ref < Nyquist.
  It will give it the frequency response the analog prototype would give to:
  
    0  <  W  =  2 tan ( ω_ref T/2 ) / T  <  +∞.

  Compared to the analog prototype, the digital implementation interprets frequency as being too high --
  -- so its frequency response is (non-linearly) shifted towards the low-end.
  
  We know that varying the cutoff shifts the curve horizontally.
  A higher cutoff shifts it towards the high-end -- that's what we want.

  So we want a higher digital cutoff. Let's call it Wc.

  We also know (from the concept of unit-cutoff normalisation) that every frequency's response is dictated by its distance to the cutoff.

  So what we want is to preserve a proportionality.
  So we want:
                                                                2       /       T \    ωc
      ω_ref / ωc  =  W / Wc    <=>    Wc  =   W ωc / ω_ref  =  --- tan ( ω_ref --- ) -------
                                                                T       \       2 /   ω_ref
                                                          
  If we use Wc instead of ωc as our cutoff, ω will preserve its frequency response despite the bilinear transform's frequency warping.
  If ω_ref = ωc, then:  ω_ref / ωc  =  1  -- then:

            2       /    T \
    Wc  =  --- tan ( ωc --- )
            T       \    2 /

  That's our general formula -- and it's actually rather clean:
    #  to pre-wrap at cutoff: anticipate the mapping on the analog cutoff.
    #  to pre-warp at <whatever>: anticipate the mapping on <whatever>, and multiply by the ratio of the analog cutoff over <whatever>.
  In both cases you get your digital cutoff.

  Jolly good. Let's make a digital High-pass filter.



