#07  Is the bilinear transform stable?
===============================================================================================

  Yes, no, maybe... we don't know.
  The analog prototype is stable when all its poles have negative real parts.
  A pole is a root of the denominator, which is a polynomial in s.
  So a pole p is a value of  s  =  σ + jω.
  So  Re (p)  <  0  means  σ < 0.
  Since  z  =  exp (sT)  =  exp (σT + jωT)  =  exp (σT) exp (jωT),  |z|  =  exp (σT)    and    arg (z)  =  jω    (Chapter I).
  And since T is always positive and exp (x) is strictly positive and strictly increasing for all real x:

    σ  <  0    <=>   σT  <  0    <=>    exp (σT)  <  1    <=>    |z|  <  1    <=>    |z|²  <  1

  The bilinear transform's substitution is:        //
