               2     1  -  1/z                     //
    s_BLT  =  --- * ------------                   //        or, equivalently, if:    Re (s_BLT)  <  0    <=>           |z|  <  1
               T     1  +  1/z                     //

     T             z - 1             T                                     T             T
    --- s_BLT  =  -------    <=>    --- s_BLT (1 + z)  =  z - 1    <=>    --- s_BLT  +  --- s_BLT z  +  1  -  z  =  0
     2             z + 1             2                                     2             2
                                                                                                                     2  +  T s_BLT
                                                                                                                    ---------------
            T                         T                                                                                    2              2  +  T s_BLT
    <=>    --- s_BLT  +  1  =  z  -  --- z    <=>    1  +  T s_BLT / 2  =  z ( 1  -  T s_BLT / 2 )    <=>    z  =  -----------------  =  ---------------
            2                         2                                                                              2  -  T s_BLT        2  -  T s_BLT
                                                                                                                    ---------------
                  2/T  +  ( σ_BLT  +  j ω_BLT )       2/T  +  σ_BLT  +  j ω_BLT                                          2
    <=>    z  =  -------------------------------  =  -----------------------------
                  2/T  -  ( σ_BLT  +  j ω_BLT )       2/T  -  σ_BLT  -  j ω_BLT

                   |  2/T  +  σ_BLT  +  j ω_BLT  |      | 2/T  +  σ_BLT  +  j ω_BLT |                      ( 2/T  +  σ_BLT )²  +  ( +ω_BLT )²
    <=>    |z|  =  | --------------------------- |  =  -------------------------------    <=>    |z|²  =  ------------------------------------
                   |  2/T  -  σ_BLT  -  j ω_BLT  |      | 2/T  -  σ_BLT  -  j ω_BLT |                      ( 2/T  -  σ_BLT )²  +  ( -ω_BLT )²

                     4/T²  +  4 σ_BLT / T  +  σ_BLT²  +  ω_BLT²         //    We want the square root of this expression to be less than one.
    <=>    |z|²  =  --------------------------------------------        //    So we want this expression to be less than one.
                     4/T²  -  4 σ_BLT / T  +  σ_BLT²  +  ω_BLT²         //    So we want the numerator inferior to the denominator.

    4/T²  +  4 σ_BLT / T  +  σ_BLT²  +  ω_BLT²  <  4/T²  -  4 σ_BLT / T  +  σ_BLT²  +  ω_BLT²        //    Subtract redundant terms from both sides.

    <=>   + 4 σ_BLT / T  <  - 4 σ_BLT / T        //    Divide both sides by T/4.

    <=>    + σ_BLT  <  - σ_BLT    <=>    σ_BLT  +  σ_BLT  <  0    <=>    2 σ_BLT  <  0    <=>    σ_BLT  <  0 / 2  =  0

  Notice that this demonstration survives if you flip the inequality or replace it with an equality. Therefore:

    Re (s)  <  0    <=>    Re (s_BLT)  <  0        &&        Re (s)  >  0    <=>    Re (s_BLT)  >  0        &&        Re (s)  =  0    <=>    Re (s_BLT)

  So: is the bilinear transform stable? -- yes, no, maybe. Depends on the analog prototype.
  Bilinear transform preserves stability, unstability and marginal stability.
  All this from failing at drawing a proper trapeze...

  Jolly good, jolly good. But what about cutoff pre-warping?
  Moving the cutoff moves the poles. True.
  But if your cutoff is strictly positive, there's nothing arctangent can do about it.

  And what if there's no analog prototype?
  Then I will slowly pronounce bi_li_ne_ar _ TRA_NS_FO_RM (emphasis on "transform").
  In that case, your starting point should likely be a function of z.
  Then check  |z|  <  1  and you're good -- that is if you want stability, otherwise just flip the inequality.



