#09  Alternative pre-warping
===============================================================================================

  Disclaimer: I didn't come up with the following idea.
  It's Robert Bristow-Johnson's technique -- and it's exposed at the end of his famous cookbook.
  That is: *after* the copy/paste-ready closed formulae, RBJ shows us where they come from: not black magic,
  only Calculus and trigonometry -- ok, maybe that's what black magic refers to.
  This means: his cookbook didn't have to be one -- had people read it in full.

  https://www.musicdsp.org/en/latest/_downloads/3e1dc886e7849251d6747b194d482272/Audio-EQ-Cookbook.txt

  (As you can see, I didn't come up with the pseudo-code monospace aesthetic either --
  -- "opposite of a cookbook"... more like "Through the Looking-Glass".)
  ((Monospace block diagrams are 100% Working-class block diagrams though.))

  Robert injects BLT into the transfer function in unit-cutoff normalisation.
  So the substitution is not operated on s, but rather on  s'  =  s / ωc.
  For it to work properly, we need s and ωc to be commensurable.
  We need to rescale  0 < ωc T < Nyquist  as  0 < ωc < +∞.
  So:
      lim    <---   lim
    ωc --> π      ωc --> +∞

  We've already found the formula (#03):        ωc  <---  2 tan ( ωc T/2 ) / T

  In case you're reading RBJ's cookbook in parallel and wonder why there is no T in his formula:
  we're using ωc, he's using w0 -- which is defined around the beginning ("Then compute a few intermediate variables"),
  as:  w0 = 2*pi*f0/Fs  which here corresponds to:

    2π fc / f_s  =  ωc / f_s  =  ωc T    then:    w0/2  corresponds to:    ωc T/2    alles gut.

  The substitution:

                    2     1 - z^-1
                   --- * ----------
                    T     1 + z^-1             1           1 - z^-1
    s'    <---    ------------------  =  -------------- * ----------
                    2                     tan (ωc T/2)     1 + z^-1
                   --- tan (ωc T/2)
                    T

  Ok, clean. Except the function tan (x) tends to infinity when x tends to π/2,
  so  1 / tan (ωc T/2)  will give very bad approximations when the frequency tends to Nyquist.
  Luckily there are legions of trigonometric identities. So we can likely re-express 1 / tan (x/2) in terms of cosine and sine.
  Except...
                 sin (x)               1          cos (x)
    tan (x)  =  ---------    so    ---------  =  ---------
                 cos (x)            tan (x)       sin (x)

  and  sin (x)  =  0  means also very bad approximations when the frequency tends to DC.
  So the ideal solution should only rely on cosine.
  Easy:  sin (x)  =  cos (x - π/2)  ...  which doesn't fix th DC issue.
  Looking back at RBJ's solution, he didn't bother with the sine issue.
  I guess when people accuse traditional BLT of quantisation noise at DC, they in fact actually know their shit...
  Or at least they trust people who do.
  EDIT:  As we're about to find out, it's more complicated than that (spoiler: denominators can cancel out...)

  Never mind.

         1           cos (x/2)
    -----------  =  -----------
     tan (x/2)       sin (x/2)

  Euler to the rescue.
  Let's define  ξ = x/2  <=>  x = 2ξ.
  Let's be bold and try solving both equations at once, using:  exp (j x)  =  cos (x)  +  j sin (x)  =  ζ.
  So:

    ζ  =  exp (j 2 ξ)  =  exp (j ξ)²    <=>    cos (2 ξ)  +  j sin (2 ξ)  =  ( cos (ξ)  +  j sin (ξ) )²

    <=>    cos (2 ξ)  +  j sin (2 ξ)  =  cos² (ξ)  +  j 2 cos (ξ) sin (ξ)  -  sin² (ξ)

