  BUT: TDF1 is a nightmare.
  It could be split in two halves (left and right) just like DF2. Which means: it also separates the poles from the zeroes --
  -- the numerator from the denominator.
  I wouldn't recommend using Transposed Direct Form I or Direct Form II.
  The pass-band amplitude is usually a factor 1.
  Which means that the numerator and the denominator compensate each other to find equilibrium at unity.
  If you split them, you'll likely multiply a very big number with a very small number (or the other way around) just to get a factor 1...
  Unless your design somehow has a numerator and a denominator both close to 1 across the whole spectrum --
  -- I don't see how this can be good for numeral approximation.


