    1917 for example.
  
    Divide it by 2, you get 958 and a remainder of 1.
    This remainder is our first bit's value.
    Notice that the remainder of an integer divided by 2 can only be 1 or 0.
    More symbolically:  if n is an integer, then  n mod 2  =  (0 || 1).
  
    Now divide 958 by 2, you get 479 and a remainder of 0.
    So our first (or last if you read left to right) two bits are:  01.
  
    Now divide 479 by 2, you get 239 and a remainder of 1.
    So:  101.
  
    Now divide 239 by 2, you get 119 and a remainder of 1.
    So:  1101.
  
    Now divide 119 by 2, you get 59 and a remainder of 1.
    So:  11101.
  
    Now divide 59 by 2, you get 29 and a remainder of 1.
    So:  111101.
  
    Now divide 29 by 2, you get 14 and a remainder of 1.
    So:  1111101.
  
    Now divide 14 by 2, you get 7 and a remainder of 0.
    So:  01111101.
  
    Now divide 7 by 2, you get 3 and a remainder of 1.
    So:  101111101.
  
